<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mortimer Wheeler</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1890">1890</a>–<a href="/years/1976">1976</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Robert Eric Mortimer Wheeler</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Mortimer Wheeler was a British archaeologist and army officer who directed the National Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> Museum and in 1934 established the Institute of Archaeology in London. He argued for a scientific and methodical approach to excavation and the recording of stratigraphic context, developing the 'Wheeler method', and excavated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verulamium">Verulamium</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> hill fort of Maiden Castle. As director-general of the Archaeological Survey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from 1944 he oversaw excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harappa">Harappa</a>, Arikamedu and Brahmagiri. His books and broadcasts brought archaeology to a mass audience, though many of his interpretations have since been discredited or reinterpreted.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mortimer-wheeler.jpg" alt="Mortimer Wheeler" width="330" height="446" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bassano Ltd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mortimer_Wheeler_in_1939.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 1940s the British archaeologist <b>Mortimer Wheeler</b> blamed invading Aryans, pointing to skeletons found in the upper levels of Mohenjo-daro, but later study showed that they were not the victims of a single massacre, and the theory of a violent conquest has been abandoned.</blockquote><a href="/culture/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>At Brahmagiri in Karnataka, <b>Mortimer Wheeler</b>’s excavation of 1947 helped establish the culture sequence of south Indian prehistory.</blockquote><a href="/culture/south-indian-megalithic-culture">South Indian megalithic culture · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
